# Syndicate Smart Contract Audit Reports

## Overview

This folder contains multiple public smart contract audit reports for **Syndicate**, an appchain infrastructure project focused on programmable onchain sequencers and custom blockchain networks.

Softstack completed **multiple separate security engagements for Syndicate** across several parts of the protocol, including token infrastructure, core protocol architecture, smart wallets, staking emissions, and earlier protocol modules.

This is a strong public example of a **recurring audit relationship** rather than a one-off engagement.

## About Syndicate

**Syndicate** is infrastructure for building and scaling appchains with programmable onchain sequencers. The protocol is designed to help developers launch custom blockchain networks tailored to specific applications and ecosystems.

That makes Syndicate relevant to one of the most important infrastructure trends in Web3: moving from generic smart contract deployments toward more specialized, application-specific chains and community-owned network infrastructure.

## Why This Project Matters

Syndicate is important because it is not just a single dApp or token launch. It is infrastructure for network creation, sequencing, staking, emissions, and appchain growth.

That makes this folder a strong public reference for:

- blockchain infrastructure security
- appchain and sequencing-related smart contract audits
- token and staking contract security
- smart wallet audits
- recurring security work across evolving protocol modules

For buyers, partners, and researchers, repeat audit work across so many separate modules is a meaningful trust signal and one of the strongest patterns visible in the public repository.

## Recurring Audit Relationship

This folder shows **multiple public audit reports across multiple dates and protocol areas**, including:

- Protocol V2
- ERC721M
- SmartWallets
- Syndicate Token
- Core Smart Contracts
- Staking Emissions

This is exactly the kind of public audit history that signals an ongoing security relationship rather than a single isolated engagement.

